#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351715 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74a902da-f299-54a3-8e93-ad2716d0e887" :knowledge-point/title "Modeling Inhibited Decay"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17b40fcc-b3c2-5322-8b07-9978fd3e24a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=k(T-30)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351717} {:answer/id #uuid "1883dfb8-e01a-5d01-b5a5-d0fecbccbfcd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-30)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351718} {:answer/id #uuid "fcf6e422-adfa-5c75-a7a4-29432cb38371" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-kT(T-30)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351719} {:answer/id #uuid "eb5b1057-687c-5226-a346-f7bc51c30a95" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T+30)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351720} {:answer/id #uuid "ab7903d2-9051-54e1-8cf9-d02e1bd2865d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(30-T)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351721}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1883dfb8-e01a-5d01-b5a5-d0fecbccbfcd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-30)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351718} :answer-field/id #uuid "b2cf2355-be7a-5273-bf10-a9293598b90e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351716}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "660e9efe-0c2c-57ea-8c8f-8be8f8b69eca" :question/math-academy-id "q-106936" :question/problem "The rate at which a pot of hot oil cools is proportional to the difference between its temperature and the temperature of its surroundings. Let $T(t)$ be the temperature of the liquid in degrees Celsius, and let $t>0$ be the time in minutes. If the temperature of the surroundings is ${30}^{∘}\\text{C},$ which differential equation could be used to model the temperature of the oil? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-{T}_{S}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${T}_{S}$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, ${T}_{S}={30}^{∘}\\text{C},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-30).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351722 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74a902da-f299-54a3-8e93-ad2716d0e887" :knowledge-point/title "Modeling Inhibited Decay"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "976ac44e-a8b2-5b1e-8351-669cd0708f87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T+75)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351724} {:answer/id #uuid "e9bd00ed-8bf5-5ac3-ae95-df096a8f185f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-75k(T-450)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351725} {:answer/id #uuid "485153c7-30e1-559a-ab90-c4afe640afd0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-450k(T-75)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351726} {:answer/id #uuid "a59d0edc-f329-5321-ac62-4cc081be745c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-75)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351727} {:answer/id #uuid "5fddd141-4fc8-5236-ae0f-472f30f397f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-450)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351728}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a59d0edc-f329-5321-ac62-4cc081be745c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-75)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351727} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0be2be1-7ea5-5a61-89e6-0a1686a41c70" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351723}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "03e9b58f-7db1-56ad-b923-db5b850905ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-106937" :question/problem "A hair straightener reaches a temperature of ${450}^{∘}\\text{F}.$ After turning it off, the temperature $T(t)$ of the straightener decreases at a rate that obeys Newton's law of cooling. Here, $T$ is measured in degrees Fahrenheit, and $t>0$ is the time in minutes. If the room temperature is ${75}^{∘}\\text{F},$ which differential equation could be used to model the straightener temperature? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-{T}_{S}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${T}_{S}$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, ${T}_{S}={75}^{∘}\\text{F},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-75).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351729 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bfe4679b-9724-5502-89d4-05e7661a1b5f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inhibited Decay Model"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "efd0d021-fbc2-5be3-bcd6-b1419ffa6f63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351731} {:answer/id #uuid "5cff09bc-54c0-5019-b5a3-eacc817ec533" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351732} {:answer/id #uuid "2aab88d7-1299-59f7-ae16-e915ede32a0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351733} {:answer/id #uuid "87ce2df6-7e54-5787-887f-6b705d17ad87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351734} {:answer/id #uuid "bf4c9b83-6249-51da-83b1-e34194030378" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351735}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "87ce2df6-7e54-5787-887f-6b705d17ad87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351734} :answer-field/id #uuid "818b37e4-1fc2-5e67-ba30-6d86a0240800" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351730}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "357c35a0-f2c3-51a5-ba26-cd42dadcc723" :question/math-academy-id "q-107122" :question/problem "A thermometer is taken out of a room where the air temperature is ${72}^{∘}F$  and brought outside where the temperature is ${15}^{∘}F,$ and its reading begins to decrease. After $2$ minutes, the thermometer reading is ${54}^{∘}F.$ To the nearest minute, how long does it take for the thermometer to register a reading of ${25}^{∘}F?$ \n\n*Hint: You may assume that the temperature reading $T(t)$ of the thermometer decreases at a rate that obeys Newton's law of cooling, where $T$ is measured in degrees Fahrenheit and $t>0$ is the time, in minutes, since the thermometer was transferred outside to ${15}^{∘}F.$*" :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-{T}_{S}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${T}_{S}$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, ${T}_{S}={15}^{∘}\\text{C},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-15).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-15} & =-k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-15} & =-k\\int dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-15| & =-kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T-15>0$ for all $t>0.$\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-15 & ={e}^{-kt+C} \\\\ T-15 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T-15 & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T & =15+K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial temperature reading was ${72}^{∘}F.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0)=72,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}72 & =15+K{e}^{0} \\\\ 72 & =15+K \\\\ K & =57\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $T(t):$\n\n$$\nT(t)=15+57{e}^{-kt}\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, after $2$ minutes, the thermometer reading is ${54}^{∘}C.$ So, applying the condition $T(2)=54,$ we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}54 & =15+57{e}^{-2k} \\\\ 39 & =57{e}^{-2k} \\\\ \\frac{39}{57} & ={e}^{-2k} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{39}{57}) & =-2k \\\\ k & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{39}{57})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the thermometer reads ${25}^{∘}F.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({t}_{1}) & =25 \\\\ 15+57{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =25 \\\\ 57{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =10 \\\\ {e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{10}{57} \\\\ -k{t}_{1} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{10}{57}) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{10}{57}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in our value for $k,$ we conclude that the thermometer will read ${25}^{∘}C$ after\n\n$$\n{t}_{1}=\\frac{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{10}{57})}{-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{39}{57})}≈9\\,\\text{min},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest minute."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078662 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bfe4679b-9724-5502-89d4-05e7661a1b5f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inhibited Decay Model"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a46df3c0-fa1b-5b99-85d9-3113025fcdf5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13\\,\\mathrm{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186075282} {:answer/id #uuid "2183fae8-7e1c-53f0-ae6f-fe74064627ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\,\\mathrm{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186075283} {:answer/id #uuid "26bc257c-8668-5575-b189-f8907154496a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14\\,\\mathrm{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186075284} {:answer/id #uuid "4b2f5d4a-867a-554f-97f8-737bce32f42b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15\\,\\mathrm{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186075285} {:answer/id #uuid "57a6e665-098b-511b-bb38-52284e1ac66e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\,\\mathrm{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186075286}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2183fae8-7e1c-53f0-ae6f-fe74064627ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\,\\mathrm{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186075283} :answer-field/id #uuid "d4238ab8-3d21-5159-b63a-92d299dc858c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186075281}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8b0ed3c5-b3aa-5668-ae2c-48b159839332" :question/math-academy-id "q-15384" :question/problem "A thermometer is placed into a water bath and displays a reading of ${50}^{∘}C.$ The thermometer is then transferred to a ${10}^{∘}C$ water bath, and its reading begins to decrease. After $5$ minutes, the thermometer reading is ${40}^{∘}C.$ To the nearest minute, how long does it take for the thermometer to register a reading of ${30}^{∘}C?$ \n\n*Hint: You may assume that the temperature reading $T(t)$ of the thermometer decreases at a rate that obeys Newton's law of cooling, where $T$ is measured in degrees Celsius and $t>0$ is the time, in minutes, since the thermometer was transferred into the ${10}^{∘}C$ water bath.*" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-{T}_{S}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${T}_{S}$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, ${T}_{S}={10}^{∘}\\text{C},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-10).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-10} & =-k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-10} & =-k\\int dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-10| & =-kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T-10>0$ for all $t>0.$\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-10 & ={e}^{-kt+C} \\\\ T-10 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T-10 & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T & =10+K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial temperature reading was ${50}^{∘}C.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0)=50,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}50 & =10+K{e}^{0} \\\\ 50 & =10+K \\\\ K & =40\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $T(t):$\n\n$$\nT(t)=10+40{e}^{-kt}\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, after $5$ minutes, the thermometer reading is ${40}^{∘}C.$ So, applying the condition $T(5)=40,$ we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}40 & =10+40{e}^{-5k} \\\\ 30 & =40{e}^{-5k} \\\\ \\frac{3}{4} & ={e}^{-5k} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{4}) & =-5k \\\\ k & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{4})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the thermometer reads ${30}^{∘}C.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({t}_{1}) & =30 \\\\ 10+40{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =30 \\\\ 40{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =20 \\\\ {e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -k{t}_{1} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in our value for $k,$ we conclude that the thermometer will read ${30}^{∘}C$ after\n\n$$\n{t}_{1}=\\frac{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})}{-\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{4})}≈12\\,\\text{min},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest minute."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078663 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bfe4679b-9724-5502-89d4-05e7661a1b5f" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inhibited Decay Model"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43c47fd2-83f1-5010-bf62-eec6b4d50979" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{85}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351737} {:answer/id #uuid "04f04c43-0890-55b0-b1a0-59732cec8c3c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{79}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351738} {:answer/id #uuid "537a6cab-cdd3-5b0c-8068-8c54658d9820" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{81}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351739} {:answer/id #uuid "37351407-c9dd-5382-9e45-1433ab1fabe6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{83}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351740} {:answer/id #uuid "5076a88a-871f-55cf-a226-6ab070e5ceec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{77}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351741}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "537a6cab-cdd3-5b0c-8068-8c54658d9820" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{81}^{∘}\\text{C}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351739} :answer-field/id #uuid "128db414-0b39-5a90-aa52-4275e1ae460f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351736}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "daa15db5-c1a1-535c-9031-79b2bb1e03e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-15595" :question/problem "The temperature $T(t),$ in degrees Celsius, of a cup of coffee can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=-0.05(T-25)\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, measured in minutes. If the initial temperature of the coffee is ${100}^{∘}C,$ calculate the temperature of the coffee after $6$ minutes. Round your answer to the nearest integer." :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "We're told that the temperature of the cup of coffee is modeled by the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=-0.05(T-25).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-25} & =-0.05\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-25} & =-\\int 0.05\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-25| & =-0.05t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T-25>0$ for all $t>0.$\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-25 & ={e}^{-0.05t+C} \\\\ T-25 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-0.05t} \\\\ T-25 & =K{e}^{-0.05t} \\\\ T & =25+K{e}^{-0.05t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial temperature of the coffee was ${100}^{∘}C.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0)=100,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}100 & =25+K{e}^{0} \\\\ 100 & =25+K \\\\ K & =75\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is\n\n$$\nT(t)=25+75{e}^{-0.05t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the temperature of the coffee after $t=6$ minutes is\n\n$$\nT(6)=25+75{e}^{-0.05\\cdot 6}≈{81}^{∘}\\text{C},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer."}]}
